// expect-exit: 0
//
// CORRECTED 2026-09-04 AT THE RE-PIN (TM-111). This header read
// `expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-034` from 2026-09-03 until now, and that was
// true of pin `950bb1d`. At pin `94874ce`, which carries the compiler's
// D-250, this file compiles and runs at exit 0. The probe's QUESTION is
// answered the other way round now, and the answer below is kept as the
// record of what it was.
//
// PROBE 05b — `#[derive(Eq)]` ON A PAYLOAD ENUM DOES NOT COMPILE.
//
// THIS FILE MUST NOT COMPILE, and what makes it worth committing is WHERE the
// diagnostic points: not at this file at all, but at `<derived-1>` -- the
// synthetic module the derive expansion writes. The user cannot open it, cannot
// fix it, and has written nothing wrong.
//
// THE DIAGNOSTIC, verbatim, from compiler commit 950bb1d:
//
//   NITPICK-TYPE-034 <derived-1>:2:73: `Part` has no built-in `==`: derive or
//   implement `Eq` and compare with `a.eq(b)`
//
// Read it twice. The derived `Eq` implementation is being told to derive `Eq`.
// The expansion compares two `Part` VALUES with `==` instead of comparing the
// tags and then the payloads, so the code the derive writes needs the trait the
// derive is writing.
//
// ISOLATED: it is the PAYLOAD that does it, and it is `Eq` alone.
//
//   #[derive(Eq)]  enum { Literal(uint16); Year4; }   -> TYPE-034, refused
//   #[derive(Eq)]  enum { Year4; Month2; }            -> compiles
//   #[derive(Eq)]  struct { int64:a; uint16:b; }      -> compiles
//   no derive,     enum { Literal(uint16); Year4; }   -> compiles
//   the other six derives on the payload enum         -> all compile
//
// AND THE ONE THAT COMPILES IS WORSE. `#[derive(Ord)]` on this same enum is
// accepted and its `cmp` IGNORES THE PAYLOAD: `Literal(7).cmp(Literal(9))`
// answers `Equal`. A refusal is a bad afternoon; a silent `Equal` between two
// different values is a wrong answer nobody looks for. Both halves are O-N10,
// reproduced with their transcripts under `defect/derive_payload_enum/`.
//
// WHY IT IS UNTESTED TERRITORY RATHER THAN A REGRESSION: no file anywhere in
// the compiler's own tree derives ANY trait on an enum with a payload. The
// derive tests cover `enum:Season`, `enum:Tag` and `enum:Level`, all
// payload-less, and `struct:Box<T>`.
//
// DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER: `FmtPart.Literal(uint16)` is the only payload-carrying
// variant in this library's whole specification set, so `ntime`'s exposure is
// one type and no rule requires either derive on it today. It is a constraint
// to record rather than a blocker -- and the constraint that matters is the
// second one, because `Ord` compiles.
mod:probe05b_derive_eq_refused;

#[derive(Eq)]
enum:Part = {
    Literal(uint16);
    Year4;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { exit 0i32; };

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
